course: everyday
lesson: 105
title: Knives, kitchen tools and cooking
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The kitchen is full of sharp edges and hot things, so helping there means learning a few safe habits before you start.
explain: A sharp knife used slowly on a chopping board is safer than a blunt one, because a blunt knife needs more force and slips.
explain: Hot pans, boiling water, steam and hot oil burn badly, and they stay hot long after the heat is off.
explain: For example, a boy in Auckland helping with pasta turns the pan handle inwards, so nobody can knock the boiling pot off the hob.
fact: Ask a grown-up before using a big knife, cut on a chopping board, curl your fingers in and cut away from your body.
fact: Never leave or reach for a knife hidden under soapy water, because nobody can see the blade.
fact: A blunt knife is not safer: it needs more force, so when it slips it cuts you.
fact: Graters, peelers and the cut edge of a tin lid take skin off fingers very easily.
fact: Before touching anything inside a blender, switch it off and unplug it, because the blades can start again.
fact: Do not use a knife to lever open a lid; ask for help, because the knife can snap or slip.
fact: Climb only on a steady step stool, never on a chair with wheels or the counter edge.
fact: Turn pan handles inwards and stand back when a lid comes off, because boiling water and steam scald.
fact: Never put water on hot oil: it explodes into a fireball of burning fat; turn off the heat and call a grown-up.
fact: A chip pan is a deep pan of hot oil for frying chips, and it can catch fire if it gets too hot.
fact: Metal pans stay hot long after they leave the hob, even if they look cold.
fact: Roll up long sleeves and tie back long hair before cooking near a flame.
fact: Raw egg and raw flour can carry germs, so cookie dough and cake batter should be baked before eating.
exercise: You want to cut an apple with a big kitchen knife. What is the safe way? | Ask a grown-up, use a board, curl fingers in and cut away from you | Hold the apple in your hand and cut towards your thumb | Cut it on the table quickly to get it done | Use the knife upside down so the blade is safe
exercise: There is a sharp knife under the soapy water in the sink. What is the danger? | Someone reaching in cannot see the blade and gets cut | The soap makes the knife go blunt | The knife will go rusty in a few minutes if it stays in water | The water makes the knife sharper
exercise: Your knife is blunt and keeps slipping on a tomato. What is true? | A blunt knife slips and needs more force, so it is not safer | A blunt knife is always safer than a sharp one | You should press much harder until it cuts | Blunt knives cannot cut skin at all
exercise: You are grating cheese and the piece is getting small. What should you do? | Stop before your fingers get near the holes | Keep going until it is all used up | Grate faster to finish sooner | Press the last bit down with your palm
exercise: The blender is blocked. What must you do before touching inside? | Switch it off and unplug it | Poke it with a spoon while it runs | Turn it to the fastest speed | Pour in water while it spins
exercise: Why should you not run your finger along the edge of an opened tin? | The cut edge is as sharp as a razor | The tin edge is covered in germs only | The tin edge is too hot to touch | Tins can give you an electric shock
exercise: How do you peel a potato safely? | Peel away from yourself, holding it firmly on a board | Peel towards your thumb as fast as you can | Hold it in the air and peel it quickly | Peel it while walking around the kitchen
exercise: A jar lid will not open. What should you do? | Ask for help instead of levering it with a knife | Push a knife under the lid and twist it hard | Bang the jar on the edge of the counter | Hit the lid with the blade of a knife
exercise: The snacks are on a high shelf. What is safest? | Use a steady step stool or ask someone tall | Stand on a chair with wheels | Climb onto the counter and reach up | Stand on a pile of books
exercise: You are helping at the hob with boiling pasta. What is a key rule? | Turn the pan handles inwards, away from the edge | Leave the handles sticking out to grab easily | Stand close and lean over the steam | Lift the lid towards your face to look
exercise: The oil in the frying pan starts to smoke. What do you do? | Turn off the heat and call a grown-up | Pour a cup of water into the pan | Carry the pan to the sink to cool it | Blow on the oil to cool it down
exercise: Why is water on hot oil so dangerous? | It explodes into a fireball of burning fat | It makes the oil turn into ice | It makes the pan go rusty at once and spoils it for good | It only makes the oil taste bad
exercise: A pan has just come off the hob and looks cool. What should you do? | Treat it as hot and leave it where it is | Pick it up with bare hands to move it | Touch it quickly to test it | Put it straight into cold water yourself
exercise: You have long loose sleeves and you want to stir a pot on the gas. What first? | Roll up your sleeves and tie back long hair | Stir quickly so the sleeves stay out | Hold your sleeve with the other hand | Turn the flame up high so you can finish the stirring faster
exercise: You want to taste the soup from the spoon in the pot. What is right? | Let it cool on a clean spoon before tasting | Lick the stirring spoon clean and then put it back in the pot | Drink straight from the edge of the pot | Taste it while it is still bubbling
exercise: Why should you wait before eating raw cookie dough? | Raw egg and raw flour can carry germs | Raw dough is always poisonous to people | Raw dough has more sugar than cookies | Raw dough makes your teeth fall out
test: Harry in Leeds sees his little brother reach into the soapy sink. Why should Harry warn him if knives are in there? | The blade is hidden under the bubbles | The soap is too hot for small hands | The knives might get lost down the drain | His brother might drink the water
test: Which is true about blunt knives? | They slip because you push harder | They cannot hurt anyone, however hard you push | They are better for children | They never need sharpening
test: Zoe's spoon gets stuck in the plugged-in blender. What must happen first? | The blender is switched off and unplugged | She pulls the spoon out quickly | She turns the blender on to free it | She shakes the whole blender hard to get it loose
test: Why should a step stool be used rather than a swivel chair, a turning chair on wheels? | A chair with wheels can roll away and make you fall | Chairs are not strong enough for anyone | Step stools are always taller and stronger than any chair | Chairs scratch the kitchen floor
test: A pot of soup is on the hob and its handle sticks out over the edge. What could happen? | Someone knocks it and is scalded by the soup | The plastic handle melts and drips onto the floor | The soup boils more slowly | Nothing, as handles do not move
test: A pan of oil catches fire. Which action is dangerous? | Throwing water on it | Turning off the heat | Calling a grown-up | Keeping well back from it
test: Why is it a bad idea to put the tasting spoon back in the pot? | Germs from your mouth spread to everyone's food | The spoon will melt in the pot | It makes all the food in the pot taste far too salty | It cools down the whole pot
test: Your friend wants to eat the cake batter before it is baked. What is the reason to wait? | Raw egg and flour may carry germs that make you ill | Batter tastes much better when it has been baked, so wait | Batter is too sticky to swallow | Batter has no sugar until it is baked
test: What makes a grater dangerous near the end of a piece of cheese? | Your fingertips get close to the sharp holes | The cheese gets too hard to grate | The grater gets hot as you use it and can burn your fingers | The cheese falls on the floor
test: chip pan's on fire!! chuck water on it | No, turn off the heat and call a grown-up | Yes, water puts out any fire, so throw a bucket | Throw a cup of flour on it | Carry the pan outside quickly
test: blender's stuck, gonna poke it with my fingers | Switch it off and unplug it first | Just keep it switched on and poke | Poke it with a fork while it runs | Shake the blender while it is on
test: blunt knife's safer for kids innit | No, it needs more force and slips | Yes, blunt knives cannot cut | Yes, blunt knives never slip | Yes, if you press really hard it cuts fine
test: knife's in the sink under all the bubbles somewhere, can u grab it | Never reach for a knife you cannot see | Feel around slowly with your hand for the handle | Grab quickly before it sinks | Swish the water to find it
explain: A boy in Ohio tried to cut a bagel by holding it in his palm; the knife slipped and he needed stitches in his hand.
explain: A girl in Leeds left a pan handle sticking out over the edge of the cooker; her little brother pulled it and was scalded on his arm.
fact: Many kitchen cuts happen when people cut towards their hand or hold food in their palm.
fact: For a burn or scald, cool it under cool running water for twenty minutes and get an adult; never put butter or ice on it.
fact: Hot drinks and pans pulled from the edge of tables and cookers are a common cause of serious burns in small children.
test: cutting a bagel in my hand, quick way | No, put it flat on a board and cut away from your fingers | Hold it tighter in your hand so it doesn't slip | Cut towards you, it gives you more control | Use a blunt knife in your hand
test: pan handle sticking out over the edge, whatever | Turn it in so no one knocks or pulls it | Leave it, it's easier to grab when it sticks out | Hang a towel on it | Put it right at the front
test: lil cuz pulled the hot tea off the table onto his arm | Cool it under running water for 20 minutes and get an adult | Put butter on it | Put ice on it | Wrap it up tight in a towel and carry on
test: did u hear, Sophie's little bro pulled a pan off the cooker n got boiling water all down his arm, they say he'll have scars forever. what's the real lesson | Turn pan handles inwards so little ones cannot pull them down | Small children should never be allowed in any house where there is a cooker at all | Pans are only dangerous when the water is cold | Boiling water burns are always healed in a day
test: my uncle reckons a lady put water on a chip pan fire n blew up the whole street. what's probably exaggerated | The whole street; water on burning oil does make a fireball | That water on burning oil is dangerous and can make the fire much worse | That chip pans can catch fire at all | Nothing, water on oil always flattens a street
test: someone said Jack cut his hand slicing a bagel in his palm n needed 30 stitches, n he cried like a baby lol, shall i tell everyone | No, mocking him is unkind; the lesson is cut on a board | Yes, because crying over a little cut on his hand is funny | Yes, but only the bit about him crying | Yes, so Jack learns to be tougher
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a blunt knife is considered more hazardous than a sharp one. | It requires greater force and is therefore liable to slip | It conveys germs more readily than a sharp blade | It is heavier and is therefore more likely to be dropped | It cannot be used upon a chopping board at all without damaging the board
exam: Whereas a pan of oil has caught fire, determine which action must on no account be taken. | Pouring water upon the burning oil | Turning off the heat beneath the pan | Calling an adult to the kitchen at once | Leaving the kitchen and calling for help
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning raw cookie dough. | Raw egg and raw flour may carry germs, so it is baked before eating | Raw dough is safe to eat provided that it has been kept in the refrigerator overnight | Raw dough is harmful only when it contains chocolate | Raw flour is safe, whereas only raw sugar carries germs
exam: Pursuant to the cleaning of a blender, ascertain the precaution required before the blades are touched. | It must be switched off and unplugged | It must be filled with cold water first | It must be left running at a low speed | It must be placed upon a dry tea towel
